From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: prospero-win <prospero-win@invalid>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] server, ntdll: Park files opened for writing, and locked
 files, on PS5, and lend descriptors only until they are closed.

Patch 0993 parks read-only files to keep a PS5 title under its limit of
about 249 open files, but not a file opened for writing or a file that
any handle has locked. A Minecraft mod (Distant Horizons) keeps a SQLite
database per dimension and opens it once per thread and repository. Each
connection holds the database and its WAL file open for reading and
writing, and in WAL mode it keeps a shared lock on the database for as
long as it is open. While a world loaded, 157 of the title's 239 files
were held that way (1002's log: write=157 live=0), and the game closed
with "Too many open files".

- A regular file opened for writing is tracked like a read-only one, and
  opened again with its access mode but without O_CREAT, O_EXCL or
  O_TRUNC. One that can no longer be opened for writing by its name (a
  file created with the read-only attribute, for one) is not parked.
- Locks no longer keep a descriptor. Every Wine process of a title runs
  in the title's host process or on its server (patch 1011), and only
  the server takes host locks. Host record locks belong to a process, so
  they never exclude anyone there, and closing any descriptor of a file
  drops all of them. The server now takes none, and its own list of
  locks, which stays with the fd, decides who may lock what. A handle
  closed while others hold locks on its file is closed at once instead
  of being kept until the last lock goes.
- A descriptor handed to the client uncached shares its file position
  with the server's. 0993 left one handed out in the last 100 ms live,
  but only measured from the first time, and a call can take longer: a
  parked file then saved a stale position, and with files opened for
  writing the next write would overwrite data. Each such descriptor is
  now a loan: get_handle_fd returns its number, and ntdll gives it back
  with the new release_fd_loan request when it closes its copy
  (server_put_unix_fd, at every place that closed one), or at once when
  the descriptor didn't arrive. A loan holds a reference to its fd, so a
  handle closed or reused in the meantime gives back nothing else, and a
  file with a loan out is never parked. A descriptor
  wine_server_handle_to_fd hands to its caller is never given back,
  which keeps that file open, as does a loan of a second process (patch
  1011) that exits holding it.
- A handle opened for writing keeps the access it was opened with only
  while it is live: one parked after its file was made read-only by
  another handle fails to open it again.
- release_fd_loan is the last request in protocol.def. A request added
  in the middle renumbers every request after it, and a module built
  without this patch (ntoskrnl.exe, conhost.exe) would send the wrong
  ones: ntoskrnl.exe asked for get_kernel_object_ptr and got another
  request, and every game that hosts the controller's HID crashed at
  start.

tests/test_wine_written_files.py opens one database 300 times for
reading and writing with a shared lock through each handle, writes 400
files through their file pointers in rounds and 40 created read-only,
maps a file for writing, checks that locks of parked handles still
conflict, also after another handle of the file is closed, and has
eight threads append records through their file pointers while two
others keep opening files. Under a host Wine and a 512-descriptor
limit, a server with 0993 fails at the 244th connection with
STATUS_TOO_MANY_OPENED_FILES (write=244); one that parks files opened
for writing but keeps the lock checks fails there too (live=244); one
without the write-access check fails to write a read-only file again
(error 5). With a 100 ms grace in place of the loans and budgets of 4
files, one run in nine lost records; with the loans, twelve runs passed,
nine of them with those budgets.
